    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d482">

                <pb facs="0715" n="701" sameAs="frus:frus1947v05/pg701"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">881.00/7–3047: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Egypt</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Patterson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-07-30T10:00:00+02:00">July 30, 1947—10
                            a. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>907. Information received by Ireland<anchor n="1" xml:id="d482-f1"/> through
                    discreet conversations with Bourguiba indicates (1) Bourguiba and Abd El
                        Karim<anchor n="2" xml:id="d482-f2"/> continue dominate local North African
                    nationalists; (2) they agree North Africans will not move toward opening Maghreb
                    office America or appealing UN until return Azzam Pasha here; (3) Bourguiba
                    holds presentations NA case to UN not probable
                    until 1948; (4) Abd El Karim does not intend visit US soon (Deptel 1055, July 17, Embtel 899, July 28<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d482-f3"/>); (5) Bourguiba will visit America only in
                    accordance advice Azzam Pasha but privately wishes visit October; (6) plans
                    Torres and Al Fassi<anchor n="4" xml:id="d482-f4"/> visit US cancelled (Embtel
                    889, July 25<anchor n="5" xml:id="d482-f5"/>); (7) arrival Moncef Bey<anchor
                        n="6" xml:id="d482-f6"/> in Egypt cannot be ruled out. Memo conversations by
                        pouch.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d482-f7"/>
                </p>
                <p>Repeated Paris and Tangier.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Patterson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d482-f1" n="1" xml:id="d482-f1-content">
                <p>Philip W. Ireland, First Secretary of Embassy in Egypt.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f2" n="2" xml:id="d482-f2-content">
                <p>Mohammed Ben Emir Abd-el Krim, Moroccan Rif leader who escaped at Port Said from
                    long-time exile while en route from Reunion to France in early June.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f3" n="3" xml:id="d482-f3-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f4" n="4" xml:id="d482-f4-content">
                <p>Abdul Khalek Torres, president of the Spanish Moroccan Reform Party, and Si Allal
                    El Fassi, leader of Moroccan Istiqlal Party.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f5" n="5" xml:id="d482-f5-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f6" n="6" xml:id="d482-f6-content">
                <p>Moncef had been deposed in May 1943 and interned in France.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d482-f7" n="7" xml:id="d482-f7-content">
                <p>None printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
